<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal Navy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal Navy is the naval service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which developed from the English navy into the most powerful fleet in the world between the eighteenth and early twentieth centuries. It protected trade routes, defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> and projected power to colonies across the globe. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> treat a professional Royal Navy as a foundation of British imperial expansion and commercial dominance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/royal-navy.jpg" alt="Royal Navy" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naval_ensign_of_the_United_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Dutch Republic — whose East India Company (VOC) long outperformed its English counterpart — and by the gradual development of the fiscal-military state that would underpin British global power: the Navigation Acts (from 1651), the Bank of England (1694), the funded national debt and a professional <b>Royal Navy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A morganatic branch, the Battenbergs, produced Alexander, the first Prince of Bulgaria, and Louis of Battenberg, First Sea Lord of the <b>Royal Navy</b>, whose descendants took the name Mountbatten.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>British power rested above all on the <b>Royal Navy</b>, the largest in the world for most of the century, financed by excise taxes and a public debt managed through the Bank of England.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thereafter the kingdom relied on diplomacy, for it could not resist foreign warships: in 1843 Lord George Paulet of the <b>Royal Navy</b> occupied the islands for five months, and in 1849 French troops seized the fort at Honolulu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1877 Barghash commissioned Lloyd Mathews, a lieutenant of the <b>Royal Navy</b>, to raise a regular force trained on European lines, which grew to more than a thousand men and later formed the core of the protectorate’s armed forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1922 the <b>Royal Navy</b> accepted parity with the United States under the Washington Naval Treaty, and the Royal Air Force, created in 1918, policed parts of the empire from the air.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
